munity · Discussion #42141\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/orgs/community/discussions/42141\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of first-time contributor guides and examples for open source on GitHub:\n\n## Key Resources for First-Time Contributors\n\n### Popular Guides and Projects\n\nThe First Contributions repository helps with copying project contents to your computer, and First Contributions allows you to make your first open source contribution in 5 minutes with a tool and tutorial to help beginners get started.\n\nThe Open Source Guides provide a guide to making open source contributions for first-timers and veterans.\n\n### Finding Issues to Work On\n\nMany open source projects use the \"good first issue\" or \"help wanted\" labels to make it easier for new contributors to find issues to work on. Every open source project has a /contribute page that highlights beginner-friendly issues, accessible by adding /contribute to the repository URL (for example https://github.com/facebook/react/contribute).\n\n### Ways to Contribute\n\nYou can look through the issue tracker to find bugs that need fixing, feature requests, or documentation improvements. Documentation improvements are almost always welcome and can be an excellent way to start contributing while learning the project.\n\n### Best Practices\n\nWhen first contributing, starting with minor fixes like documentation improvements or small bug reports can help you familiarize yourself with the codebase and contributor workflow. The standard workflow involves fork → clone → edit → pull request.\n\n### Other Resources\n\nUp For Grabs contains projects with beginner-friendly issues, and First Timers Only is a list of bugs labeled \"first-timers-only\".\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7c339905-6cc7-4364-b614-e63ff8658a48","timestamp":"2026-05-12T15:15:47.871Z","toolUseResult":{"query":"first-time con