ribute-to-open-source/blob/main/README.md\"}]\n\nI've found comprehensive information about \"first time contributors\" guides, README files, and onboarding for open source projects. Here's what the search results revealed:\n\n## Key Components for First-Time Contributor Onboarding\n\n**README Files Are Critical:**\nThe README file should be your first stop, and serves as a project's landing page. Projects with a README file show a 55% productivity boost.\n\n**What an Effective README Should Include:**\nA welcoming and powerful README.md file includes: a welcoming note, presentation of the project, explanation of what makes it unique, current status, step-by-step instructions on how to get started, basic use cases and examples, description of needed contributions, pointers to key resources like CONTRIBUTING.md and LICENSE.md, and contact information.\n\n**Documentation's Impact:**\nDocumentation is one of the most helpful ways to onboard new contributors. Contribution guidelines are one of the most important types of documentation every project should have: the Octoverse 2021 report found that repositories that include contribution guidelines are 17% more productive.\n\n**Additional Essential Files:**\nWhereas READMEs help people use the project, contributing docs help people contribute to the project. It explains what types of contributions are needed and how the process works.\n\n**Best Practices for Setup:**\nProjects should add a section to documentation that outlines common problems people have installing the software the first time, and create a quick-start guide with install scripts that reduce the number of steps required to get started.\n\n**Labeling Issues:**\nTag about 25% of your issues as \"Good First Issues\" and watch new contributors jump by 13%.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8f7c7e55-ec9a-43d3-84c3-41e662bbe18e","timestamp":"2026-05-12T15:15:48.741Z","toolUseResu