{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-05-12T15:20:04.667Z","sessionId":"8060bbd7-614d-4663-826b-acfc58102db6","content":"okay so i want to use Community Health Toolkit: First Time Contributors Guide, STUMPY Contributing Guide , and beets as our example for creating a first time contributors guide\n\nYep, these are much closer to what you’re looking for:\n\n- [First Contributions](https://github.com/firstcontributions/first-contributions) — pure beginner walkthrough for making a first PR. Great model for the mechanical `fork → clone → branch → edit → PR` flow.\n- [Git: My First Contribution](https://git.github.io/htmldocs/MyFirstContribution.html) — very detailed project-specific “first patch” guide. Good if you want to walk someone through one realistic contribution.\n- [Wagtail: Your first contribution](https://docs.wagtail.org/en/7.3/contributing/first_contribution_guide.html) — strong on social workflow: picking issues, commenting before work, avoiding duplicated effort, asking for help.\n- [Community Health Toolkit: First Time Contributors Guide](https://docs.communityhealthtoolkit.org/community/contributing/first-time-contributors/) — a full newcomer path: who it’s for, first issue, fork, clone, dev environment, commit, push, PR.\n- [BeeWare: First-time contributors](https://beeware.org/contributing/first-time/) — friendly tone, beginner anxiety, GitHub basics, environment setup, glossary.\n- [pyOpenSci: Your First Open Source Contribution](https://www.pyopensci.org/lessons/contribute-open-source/your-first-contribution.html) — lesson-style and docs-first, nice for reducing command-line intimidation.\n- [STUMPY Contributing Guide](https://stumpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/Contribute.html) — not standalone, but has a good first-time contributor framing and even a sample “first time contributor here” issue comment.\n- [beets: Your First Contribution](https://beets.readthedocs.io/en/stable/contributing.html#your-first-contribution) — compact example of embedding a first-time section inside a normal contributing guide.\n\nFor Entire, I’d probably borrow the structure from CHT/Wagtail, the warmth from BeeWare, and the practical “here’s exactly what to type / what to comment” style from First Contributions and STUMPY."}
